    "raw_standard_answer": null,
    "resolved_question_text": "The figure is a microscopic image of a diploid rice pollen mother cell at a certain stage of meiosis. How many chromosomes does this cell contain at this time?",
    "resolved_answer": "24 chromosomes",
    "resolved_from_fallback": true,
    "final_cot": "First describe the image: In the microscopic image, the background is relatively dark, and multiple groups of condensed bright-colored chromosome structures can be seen inside the cell; the position indicated by the arrow is labeled “tetrad,” indicating that these paired condensed structures are tetrads formed by synapsis of homologous chromosomes during meiosis. The number of visible tetrads in the figure is about 12. Then reason: The stem states that this is a meiotic stage of a diploid rice pollen mother cell. The chromosome number of diploid rice is 2n=24. During prophase I of meiosis, when tetrads form, the number of tetrads equals the haploid chromosome number n=12. Each tetrad consists of a pair of homologous chromosomes, that is, contains 2 chromosomes. Therefore, this cell contains 12×2=24 chromosomes at this time.",
    "claims": [],
